    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d945">

                <pb facs="0965" n="953" sameAs="frus:frus1937v01/pg953"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">561.35E1A/15: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the United
                        Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bingham</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-07-02T18:00:00-04:00">July 2, 1937—6 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>272. Your 414, June 29, 5 p.m. Tell Council Secretary that latest draft of sugar
                    marketing legislation reported out of Agricultural Committee of House of
                        Representatives,<anchor n="5" xml:id="d945-f1"/> to replace legislation
                    which expires December 31, 1937, provides for full-duty quota from foreign
                    countries of 89 percent of 44.41 percent of requirements of consumers in
                    Continental United States; for allocation of quota among full-duty countries on
                    basis of division made in Regulations mentioned in Article 9 (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">a</hi>) of sugar agreement; for proration among them of
                    any unused part of the Philippine full-duty quota which quota on basis of
                    present estimated consumption would be about 60,000 tons; for proration among
                    them on September 1 of each year of any unused parts of their previous
                    allocation. The bill is not sponsored by executive branch and is not assured of
                    enactment.</p>
                <p>It is premature to consider contingent application of Article 9 (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">c</hi>) under conditions that have not yet arisen.</p>
                <p>Peruvian officials have been told only that it is hoped that legislation will be
                    enacted under which their participation in United States market may be
                    materially larger than it has been.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d945-f1" n="5" xml:id="d945-f1-content">
                <p>H. R. 7667, <hi rendition="#italic">Congressional Record</hi>, Vol. 81, pt. 6, p.
                    6781. The Sugar Act of 1937 was approved September 1; 50 Stat. 903.</p>
            </note>
